  "statement": "For $f(x)=x^{3}-x-1$: (i) show there is a root in $(1,2)$; (ii) using $x_{0}=1$, compute $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ by Newton-Raphson; (iii) the root is about $1.3247$; estimate the error in $x_{2}$.",
  "solution": "(i) $f(1)=-1$, $f(2)=5$; sign change, so a root lies in $(1,2)$. (ii) $f'(x)=3x^{2}-1$. $x_{1}=1-\\frac{-1}{2}=1.5$. $x_{2}=1.5-\\frac{1.5^{3}-1.5-1}{3(1.5)^{2}-1}=1.5-\\frac{0.875}{5.75}\\approx1.347826$. (iii) Error $\\approx|x_{2}-1.3247|\\approx0.0231$.",
